In Java, polymorphism is achieved through interfaces and inheritance. Here's an example of polymorphism using an interface and multiple classes that implement it:

```java
// Interface Animal
public interface Animal {
    void speak();
}

// Class Dog implementing the Animal interface
class Dog implements Animal {
    public void speak() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

// Class Cat implementing the Animal interface
class Cat implements Animal {
    public void speak() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal myPet = new Dog(); // Polymorphism allows us to treat a Dog as an Animal
        myPet.speak();            // and call its methods without knowing the specific type

        myPet = new Cat();        // Now we can treat a Cat as an Animal too
        myPet.speak();
    }
}
```

In Java, polymorphism is achieved through interfaces and inheritance. Here's an example of using polymorphism with interfaces:

```java
// Interface Animal
public interface Animal {
    void makeSound();
}

// Class Dog implementing the Animal interface
public class Dog implements Animal {
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

// Class Cat implementing the Animal interface
public class Cat implements Animal {
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

// Main method demonstrating polymorphism
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Animal animal = new Dog();
    animal.makeSound(); // Output: Woof!

    animal = new Cat();
    animal.makeSound(); // Output: Meow!
}
```
In this example, the `Animal` interface defines a method called `makeSound()`. The `Dog` and `Cat` classes both implement this interface, providing their own implementation of the `makeSound()` method. In the main method, we create instances of both `Dog` and `Cat`, assign them to an `Animal` reference variable, and call the `makeSound()` method on that variable. This demonstrates polymorphism because the same method (`makeSound()`) can be called with different implementations depending on the object's type at runtime.
